.bundle/
.DS_Store
*.db
*.db-journal
# RSpec's own personal-override convention (auto-merged with the
# committed .rspec, never meant to be shared) — a contributor's own
# `--format progress --fail-fast` while iterating, without touching
# the shared .rspec's `--format documentation` (kept for CI
# readability). See .rspec-local.example for the recommended contents.
.rspec-local
# Gemfile.lock is committed (see below) — every dependency this project
# resolves, not just json (which the Gemfile itself pins to an exact
# version), is fixed for CI. Without this file tracked, ruby/setup-ruby's
# bundler-cache generates its own lockfile from whatever the resolver
# picks on cache miss, so an unrelated gem's release can silently change
# CI's dependency graph between runs with no diff to review.
# The RUNTIME era store, which a file adapter writes under each domain's own
# `<root>/data/eras/<domain>/`. Anchored to `data/`, and deliberately: this was
# a bare `eras/`, which matches a directory of that name at ANY depth — so it
# also swallowed `spec/fixtures/eras/`, the eleven bump_/same_ bluebooks BOTH
# runtimes' storage-shape tests walk. They were never committed, nobody saw
# them missing, and the Ruby suite aborted at load rather than failing a test.
**/data/eras/
# Heki's own on-disk artifacts, never checked-in state: the flock lock
# file beside every `.heki` snapshot (created on first save, left in
# place — flock needs no cleanup), and the temp file `write` renames
# over the snapshot from (only survives a crash between the write and
# the rename).
*.heki.lock
*.heki.tmp.*
tmp/
vendor/bundle/
# `sam build`'s own local build output, under every deploy/<stack>/ —
# never committed; a stack's tracked tree holds only the files
# `hecks deploy recipe.project` itself generates.
.aws-sam/
# banking's recipe is the recipe of a live stack that belongs to the
# organisation running it, generated from its own environment overlay;
# it is kept in that organisation's repo, and a copy under this repo's
# deploy/ goes stale. The generic example is examples/banking itself.
/deploy/banking/
coverage/
.claude/*
# Skills are project knowledge, not session scratch — tracked so any
# future session on this repo (this machine or a fresh clone) gets
# them for free, the same reason docs/decisions/ is tracked. `.claude/*`
# above, not `.claude/` — a directory-matching pattern stops git from
# ever descending into it at all, so a negation below can't reach
# anything inside; the glob form still excludes every current child
# but leaves each one negatable.
!.claude/skills/
# The hooks every agent session on this repo runs (rubocop after an edit), tracked for the same reason.
!.claude/settings.json
!.claude/hooks/
